        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#who-built-them" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Who Actually Built Them</a>
          <a href="#what-they-were" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. What the Machines Actually Were</a>
          <a href="#parts" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. Getting Parts</a>
          <a href="#common-faults" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. What Goes Wrong, and the Cheap Fixes First</a>
          <a href="#worth-keeping" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Is Yours Worth Keeping?</a>
          <a href="#buying-used" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Buying One Today</a>
          <a href="#what-to-buy-instead" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. What Replaced It</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          For about twenty years, Weslo was the answer to "what is the cheapest treadmill that is
          not a toy". That made it one of the highest-volume treadmill brands in the country, and
          it is why so many people are now looking for a belt for one.
        </p>

        <div id="who-built-them" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Who Actually Built Them</h2>
<p>
            Weslo was never an independent company. It was a budget brand of <strong>ICON Health &amp;
            Fitness</strong>, which was for a long time the largest home fitness equipment
            manufacturer in the world, and which renamed itself <strong>iFIT Health &amp; Fitness</strong>
            in 2021 after the platform that had become its main business.
          </p>
          <p>
            The same company owns
            <a href="/nordictrack/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack</a> and
            <a href="/proform-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm</a>, and has
            held or licensed several other familiar fitness names. Weslo sat at the bottom of that
            structure: the entry point, sold through mass retail, built on the same production lines
            with the cheapest specification the company offered.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>That is the single most useful fact for an owner.</strong> An orphaned brand
            usually means orphaned hardware — a machine from a company that no longer exists, with
            parts nobody stocks. That is not the situation here. The manufacturer is large, still
            trading, and still making treadmills on related lines under better-known names.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Why the brand faded</strong> is straightforward commercial logic rather than
            anything dramatic. The parent's business moved decisively toward subscription-led
            machines, where the money is in recurring revenue rather than in the cheapest possible
            treadmill. A budget brand with no platform attached does not fit that model, so it was
            de-emphasised rather than formally killed. Whatever remains of it exists in the same
            structural position our
            <a href="/livestrong-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">LiveStrong guide</a>
            describes: a badge whose builder never went anywhere.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-they-were" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. What the Machines Actually Were</h2>
<p>
            Worth being honest, because nostalgia and a low price both distort this.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The Cadence series was the volume line</strong> — folding frames, small motors,
            short decks, simple LED consoles, and manual incline set by moving a pin or a foot rather
            than a motor. Our
            <a href="/weslo-cadence-g-5-9-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Cadence G
            5.9 review</a> covers the archetype in detail.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>They were walking machines sold at a price that put them in front of people who
            wanted to run.</strong> That is the honest summary and it is the source of most
            complaints about the brand. A short deck, a small motor and a light frame is a coherent
            set of choices for walking; it is an incoherent one for running, and running on them is
            what wore a great many out early.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Manual incline is the detail people forget.</strong> Stop, get off, move a pin.
            Not adjustable during a session, which rules out the incline-walking protocols that make
            walking genuinely effective — the arithmetic in our
            <a href="/treadmill-calorie-calculator/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">calorie
            guide</a> shows why that matters more than it sounds.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Build quality was appropriate to the price rather than bad.</strong> These were
            not premium machines and were not sold as such. Used for walking and maintained, plenty
            of them ran for a decade, which is a perfectly respectable outcome for what they cost.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>And one quiet advantage that has aged well:</strong> the consoles are entirely
            self-contained. No account, no app, no cloud service that can be discontinued. A
            fifteen-year-old Weslo does today exactly what it did when new — which is more than can
            be said for a lot of connected machines sold since.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="parts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Getting Parts</h2>
<p>
            Better than most owners expect, for the reason set out above.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Start with the parent company's parts operation.</strong> iFIT supports its
            brands through a common service and parts channel, and approaching it as an ICON or iFIT
            product rather than searching a quiet brand name gets a far more useful answer.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cross-reference against ProForm and NordicTrack of the same era.</strong> This
            is the key practical move. The same manufacturer built all three lines in the same
            period on related platforms, so components are frequently shared or closely related —
            rollers, motors, controller boards, incline hardware and belts. A part unlisted under a
            Weslo number is often readily available under a ProForm one. Verify by part number and
            physical dimensions rather than assuming, but the hit rate is high. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-repair/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">repair guide</a> covers
            working that cross-reference.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Generic parts cover most of what fails.</strong> Belts are sized by width and
            circumference and are widely available cut to size. Rollers, deck boards, drive belts,
            motor brushes and power switches are commodity items. Silicone lubricant is silicone
            lubricant.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Salvage is unusually good here.</strong> Because the volumes were enormous,
            identical machines are given away constantly — free or nearly free on local listings,
            usually because a console died or nobody wanted to move one. A donor machine is the only
            reliable source of a board, and on this brand you will not wait long for one to appear.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Manuals are findable.</strong> Archived PDFs circulate on manual repositories.
            Search the exact model designation rather than the brand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="common-faults" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. What Goes Wrong, and the Cheap Fixes First</h2>
<p>
            The failure pattern on these machines is consistent and heavily weighted toward things
            that cost very little to put right.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A dead console is usually not a dead console.</strong> Check the safety key and
            its magnet first — they weaken with age and account for an enormous share of apparently
            dead machines. Then the wiring loom at the folding deck's pivot, where the cable has
            flexed thousands of times and conductors break inside intact-looking insulation. Then the
            breaker on the frame and the household circuit. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-wont-turn-on/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">power fault
            guide</a> walks the full sequence, and these are ten-minute checks.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A belt that slips or hesitates under load is usually a dry deck</strong>, not a
            failing motor. Slide a hand between belt and deck: dry, hot or gritty means friction is
            high. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubrication
            guide</a> is the first thing to try on any machine that feels tired, and it transforms
            how these feel.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Belt drift</strong> is normal on a light frame and is a five-minute adjustment —
            <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">centring</a>
            and <a href="/how-tight-should-treadmill-belt-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">tensioning</a>
            cover it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The genuinely expensive failures</strong> are the motor and the controller board,
            and on these machines they are nearly always downstream of years of running against a dry
            deck rather than faults in their own right. Our
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmill-motors-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">motor
            lifespan guide</a> covers the mechanism.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Deck wear</strong> is real on a machine this old. Many decks are reversible, so
            flipping one is a free repair that restores a low-friction surface — worth checking
            before buying a new board.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="worth-keeping" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Is Yours Worth Keeping?</h2>
<p>
            A framework, because the low original price distorts this in both directions.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Almost always fix</strong> a safety key, a wiring break, lubrication-related
            symptoms, belt tension or tracking, a drive belt, or a belt and deck service. Cheap parts
            on a frame that is fundamentally sound, and a service transforms how one of these feels.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Probably fix</strong> a motor or a board <em>only</em> if you can source one from
            a donor machine or a cross-referenced part cheaply. Paying full retail plus labour for a
            board on a machine that cost a few hundred dollars new rarely makes sense — and this is
            where the arithmetic differs from a mid-range treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not fix</strong> a damaged frame, or a machine with several simultaneous
            faults, or one that has been run dry for years. Our
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lifespan
            guide</a> covers reading a cluster of faults as an ending.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The test that settles it:</strong> would you pay the repair cost for this
            machine, working, at its age, with no warranty? Given what these cost new, the honest
            answer is often no — and that is not a criticism of the machine, it is arithmetic.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>But before scrapping it, do the free checks.</strong> Safety key, loom, breaker,
            lubrication, belt tracking, deck flip. A large share of Weslo treadmills sent to the tip
            were not broken in any meaningful sense, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">troubleshooting
            guide</a> is the systematic version of that hour's work.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-used" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Buying One Today</h2>
<p>
            They are everywhere secondhand, frequently free, and whether that is an opportunity
            depends entirely on what you want it for.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The case for:</strong> a working folding treadmill for the cost of collection,
            from a manufacturer whose parts channel still exists, with a console that needs no
            account and can never be switched off remotely. For a walker with a garage, that is
            difficult to argue with.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The case against:</strong> no warranty, unknown maintenance history, electronics
            at the age where they fail, a deck probably past its service life, and a machine that was
            entry-level when new.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>How to inspect one</strong> follows our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">standard
            checklist</a>, weighted toward three things. Run every console function including the
            incline mechanism. Slide a hand under the belt — dry or gritty means it ran unlubricated
            for years and the motor has already spent much of its life. And walk on it at speed to
            confirm the belt does not hesitate under load.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Price it at scrap value plus a service.</strong> Budget for a belt, lubricant and
            a couple of hours immediately. If the asking price plus that work approaches what a
            known-good budget machine costs, the arithmetic has stopped working — our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-under-500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-$500
            field</a> and our
            <a href="/xterra-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">XTERRA guide</a> are the
            comparisons.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>And take the free one over the cheap one.</strong> At this end, a free machine
            you service yourself is almost always better value than a hundred-dollar machine of
            unknown history.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-buy-instead" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. What Replaced It</h2>
<p>
            Nothing occupies exactly the position Weslo did — the very cheapest folding treadmill
            from a major manufacturer — but three things divide up the space.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>ProForm's entry machines</strong> are the closest descendant from the same
            parent, and our
            <a href="/proform-carbon-tl-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Carbon TL
            review</a> covers the archetype: a walking deck sold at a price that puts it in front of
            runners, with a platform attached you can ignore.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>XTERRA</strong> is arguably the true successor in market position — cheap, sold
            through mass retail, with a real parts channel behind it. Our
            <a href="/xterra-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">XTERRA guide</a> makes
            that case.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Walking pads took the rest.</strong> A great many people who would once have
            bought a cheap folding treadmill now buy a slim deck instead, and for walkers it is
            frequently the better machine — our
            <a href="/walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">category guide</a> covers the
            trade, and it is the single biggest structural change in the budget end of this market.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>And the used market, which Weslo itself now supplies.</strong> Our
            <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used analysis</a>
            shows secondhand buys far more hardware per dollar, and at the bottom of the market that
            gap is at its widest.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who makes Weslo treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Weslo was a budget brand of ICON Health & Fitness, which renamed itself iFIT Health & Fitness in 2021. The same company owns NordicTrack and ProForm. That is the most useful fact for an owner: the manufacturer is large, still trading, and still building treadmills on related lines, so parts and cross-references genuinely exist.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why did Weslo treadmills disappear?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Commercial logic rather than anything dramatic. The parent company's business moved decisively toward subscription-led machines where the revenue is recurring, and a budget brand with no platform attached does not fit that model. It was de-emphasised rather than formally killed — nothing was recalled and nothing was defective.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where can I get parts for a Weslo treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Start with iFIT's parts operation, approaching it as an ICON or iFIT product rather than searching the quiet brand name. Then cross-reference against ProForm and NordicTrack machines of the same era, since the same manufacturer built all three on related platforms — verify by part number and dimensions. Belts, rollers and drive belts are generic. Free donor machines are plentiful.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a Weslo treadmill worth repairing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always for a safety key, wiring, lubrication, belt tension or a belt and deck service — cheap parts on a sound frame. For a motor or board, only if you can source one from a donor or a cross-referenced part cheaply, since paying full retail plus labour rarely makes sense on a machine that cost a few hundred new. Always do the free checks first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you run on a Weslo treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They were walking machines sold at a price that put them in front of people who wanted to run, and that mismatch is the source of most complaints about the brand. Short decks, small motors and light frames are coherent choices for walking and incoherent for running — and running on them is what wore a great many out early.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I take a free Weslo treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a walker with a garage, usually yes. You get a working folding treadmill for the cost of collection, from a manufacturer whose parts channel still exists, with a console that needs no account and cannot be switched off remotely. Budget for a belt, lubricant and a couple of hours of service immediately, and inspect it properly before moving it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes with a Weslo</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The first two send working machines to the tip, which is the outcome this page exists to prevent.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the quiet brand means no parts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The manufacturer never went anywhere — ICON, now iFIT, still builds NordicTrack and ProForm, and components were frequently shared across those lines in the same period. Search by part number and by the ProForm or NordicTrack equivalent rather than by a brand name that has gone quiet, and most Weslo repairs become ordinary.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Condemning the console before the ten-minute checks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Safety key magnets weaken with age, and the wiring loom at a folding deck’s pivot flexes thousands of times until a conductor breaks inside intact-looking insulation. Both present exactly like dead electronics and both cost very little. Rule them out, along with the frame breaker, before concluding anything expensive has failed.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying one to run on because it is cheap</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A short deck, a small motor and a light frame make a coherent walking machine and an incoherent running one. Running on these is what wore most of them out early, and it is the reason the brand has a worse reputation than the hardware deserves. If running is the plan, deck length is where the money has to go.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying retail for a board on a machine that cost a few hundred new</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The repair arithmetic is different at this end of the market. A board plus labour can exceed what the machine is worth working. Given the volumes Weslo sold, an identical donor machine is usually available free or nearly free on local listings within weeks — that is the realistic source for electronics, not a parts desk.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

